// A polymorphic helper called at both a fixed type and a parameter-derived
// one, inside a function that is itself used at two types.
//
// The compiled backends refuse this, and the refusal is the point of the
// case: until v0.1.179 they emitted it, wrongly and in silence. `hold` was
// monomorphized into an `int` copy and a `str` copy; both declarations got
// the right signature, and both bodies got the `int` one's operations. The
// generated C was a `mere_vec_str*` function calling `mere_vec_int_push`,
// which is not a wrong answer but no answer — it only failed when the C
// compiler saw it, with a message about pointer conversions naming nothing
// in this file.
//
// The mechanism: codegen keeps a pristine clone of every polymorphic
// skeleton and unifies a fresh copy with each instantiation's arrow. When
// the clone cannot take the arrow the spec is wrong, and that failure was
// swallowed. It cannot happen while the skeleton is genuinely polymorphic
// — and here it is not. The typer hands codegen `hold` already fixed at
// `int -> Vec[__heap, int]`, so the `str` instance has nothing to unify
// with. Written the other way (`hold v, hold v`), the skeleton arrives
// polymorphic and both instances are correct; that shape is exercised by
// contrib/state's store and the other examples.
//
// So this file locks in the refusal, not the behaviour. The real fix is
// upstream of codegen — whatever fixes the skeleton before it is cloned —
// and when it lands, this case starts producing `1s2` on all four
// backends and the expectation here changes with it.
//
// Reduced from contrib/state/store, which has to write its three one-slot
// vecs out longhand instead of using contrib/state/cell for this reason:
// a store instantiated at two state types calls `cell_new` at a type
// derived from S and at plain `int` for its token counter.

let hold = fn (v) -> let c = vec_new () in let _ = vec_push c v in c;
let peek = fn (c) -> vec_get c 0;

// `hold v` is parameter-derived; `hold 1` is fixed. Both in one body.
let mk = fn (v) -> (hold v, hold 1);

let (a1, a2) = mk 1 in
let (b1, b2) = mk "s" in
let _ = print (show (peek a1) ++ peek b1 ++ show (peek a2 + peek b2)) in
0
